1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:05,575
Every time you spend one hundred dollars
with a seller on Amazon...

2
00:00:05,615 --> 00:00:09,516
about fifty-two of those dollars never
reach the seller.

3
00:00:09,556 --> 00:00:10,993
And in 2026, the U.S.

4
00:00:11,033 --> 00:00:16,623
government accused Amazon of secretly
overcharging the businesses that advertise

5
00:00:16,663 --> 00:00:19,719
there — by more than twenty billion
dollars.

6
00:00:19,759 --> 00:00:20,916
Amazon denies it.

7
00:00:20,956 --> 00:00:24,434
But according to the FTC, you're paying
for it too

8
00:00:24,474 --> 00:00:26,897
— even if you never shop on Amazon.

9
00:00:26,937 --> 00:00:30,908
In 2025, one line in Amazon's annual
report — advertising

10
00:00:30,948 --> 00:00:34,497
— brought in sixty-eight point six billion
dollars.

11
00:00:34,537 --> 00:00:37,030
So let's follow one hundred dollars.

12
00:00:37,070 --> 00:00:38,367
Dollar by dollar...

13
00:00:38,407 --> 00:00:41,323
and find out who actually gets your money.

14
00:00:41,713 --> 00:00:44,330
Meet a typical clothing seller on Amazon.

15
00:00:44,370 --> 00:00:48,478
For every hundred dollars in sales, here's
where the money goes.

16
00:00:48,518 --> 00:00:50,164
First, the product itself.

17
00:00:50,204 --> 00:00:53,275
Manufacturing, ocean freight, and import
duties.

18
00:00:53,315 --> 00:00:56,969
Twenty-eight dollars — paid months before
anything sells.

19
00:00:57,359 --> 00:01:00,143
The moment a customer clicks "Buy Now"...

20
00:01:00,183 --> 00:01:02,003
Amazon takes its first cut.

21
00:01:02,043 --> 00:01:05,103
A seventeen percent referral fee on
clothing.

22
00:01:05,143 --> 00:01:06,343
Seventeen dollars.

23
00:01:06,733 --> 00:01:08,862
Next, Fulfillment by Amazon.

24
00:01:08,902 --> 00:01:11,806
Picking, packing, shipping, storage...

25
00:01:11,846 --> 00:01:14,517
plus a new surcharge added in 2026.

26
00:01:14,557 --> 00:01:16,144
About twenty dollars.

27
00:01:16,534 --> 00:01:18,709
Then, the line shoppers never see.

28
00:01:18,749 --> 00:01:19,491
Advertising.

29
00:01:19,531 --> 00:01:21,706
Just to show up where buyers look,

30
00:01:21,746 --> 00:01:26,201
a typical seller spends around fifteen
dollars on sponsored listings.

31
00:01:26,591 --> 00:01:28,962
Returns and write-offs take another ten.

32
00:01:29,351 --> 00:01:30,301
The final split?

33
00:01:30,341 --> 00:01:35,314
The seller — who designed the product,
paid the factory, and carried all the risk

34
00:01:35,354 --> 00:01:36,923
— keeps about ten dollars.

35
00:01:36,963 --> 00:01:41,070
Amazon — without owning a single thread —
collects about fifty-two.

36
00:01:41,110 --> 00:01:43,731
More than five times what the seller
keeps.

37
00:01:43,771 --> 00:01:46,392
But hold on to that fifteen-dollar ad
line.

38
00:01:46,432 --> 00:01:48,682
That's where this story gets strange.

39
00:01:50,672 --> 00:01:54,257
How did an online bookstore become a toll
collector?

40
00:01:54,297 --> 00:01:57,603
The answer is buried in Amazon's SEC Form
ten-K,

41
00:01:57,643 --> 00:02:01,228
under a line called "advertising
services." In 2025,

42
00:02:01,268 --> 00:02:04,784
it brought in sixty-eight point six
billion dollars

43
00:02:04,824 --> 00:02:07,642
— up twenty-two percent in a single year.

44
00:02:07,682 --> 00:02:08,897
For perspective...

45
00:02:08,937 --> 00:02:14,543
that's more than half the combined revenue
of every daily and weekly newspaper on

46
00:02:14,583 --> 00:02:14,962
Earth.

47
00:02:15,002 --> 00:02:19,005
Not all of it comes from sellers —
streaming ads count too

48
00:02:19,045 --> 00:02:23,885
— but sponsored listings are widely
estimated to be the biggest piece.

49
00:02:24,275 --> 00:02:25,524
Here's how it works.

50
00:02:25,564 --> 00:02:30,679
Depending on the survey, between half and
two-thirds of American online shoppers

51
00:02:30,719 --> 00:02:34,030
now start their product searches directly
on Amazon.

52
00:02:34,070 --> 00:02:36,736
And once the buyers are all in one room...

53
00:02:36,776 --> 00:02:39,894
whoever controls the shelf controls the
business.

54
00:02:40,283 --> 00:02:42,921
So Amazon turned the shelf into an
auction.

55
00:02:42,961 --> 00:02:46,657
Try it yourself: search for almost
anything on your phone...

56
00:02:46,697 --> 00:02:50,766
and count the "Sponsored" results before
the first one that isn't.

57
00:02:50,806 --> 00:02:54,814
Every slot is a seller bidding, per click,
against other sellers.

58
00:02:55,204 --> 00:03:00,388
And according to a new lawsuit, even the
auction may not be what it seems.

59
00:03:00,428 --> 00:03:04,483
In August 2026, the FTC and twenty-two
states sued Amazon,

60
00:03:04,523 --> 00:03:10,130
alleging that since 2019 it secretly added
a surcharge on top of auction prices.

61
00:03:10,170 --> 00:03:15,919
Documents unsealed in November allege prices
up to fifty percent above the auction

62
00:03:15,959 --> 00:03:17,755
result on ordinary days...

63
00:03:17,795 --> 00:03:22,202
and up to two hundred and fifty percent on
days like Prime Day.

64
00:03:22,242 --> 00:03:27,144
The FTC puts the total overcharge at more
than twenty billion dollars.

65
00:03:27,184 --> 00:03:29,403
Amazon calls the suit misguided,

66
00:03:29,443 --> 00:03:33,921
and says it never charges more than an
advertiser's maximum bid.

67
00:03:34,311 --> 00:03:38,287
For sellers, it's a prisoner's dilemma:
bid away your margin...

68
00:03:38,327 --> 00:03:39,115
or disappear.

69
00:03:39,155 --> 00:03:42,366
So why don't they just charge less
everywhere else?

70
00:03:42,406 --> 00:03:43,769
Because if they try...

71
00:03:43,809 --> 00:03:44,916
a bot is watching.

72
00:03:46,906 --> 00:03:51,857
In 2023, the Federal Trade Commission and
a group of states sued Amazon for

73
00:03:51,897 --> 00:03:54,053
illegally maintaining a monopoly.

74
00:03:54,093 --> 00:03:59,110
The complaint describes what happens when
a seller tries to undercut Amazon.

75
00:03:59,150 --> 00:04:02,903
Say you sell a dress for one hundred
dollars on Amazon...

76
00:04:02,943 --> 00:04:07,495
and eighty-five on your own Shopify store,
where your fees are lower.

77
00:04:07,885 --> 00:04:13,025
According to the FTC, Amazon's algorithms
scan the internet for lower prices.

78
00:04:13,065 --> 00:04:16,321
If they find one, your offer can lose the
Buy Box

79
00:04:16,361 --> 00:04:18,676
— that yellow "Add to Cart" button.

80
00:04:18,716 --> 00:04:21,770
Industry estimates — never confirmed by
Amazon

81
00:04:21,810 --> 00:04:25,403
— say more than eighty percent of sales go
through it.

82
00:04:25,443 --> 00:04:27,219
The complaint goes further.

83
00:04:27,259 --> 00:04:32,803
It says Amazon can bury discounting sellers so
far down in search results that they

84
00:04:32,843 --> 00:04:37,849
become — quote — "effectively invisible."
So sellers do the rational thing.

85
00:04:37,889 --> 00:04:42,894
In the FTC's words, they use their
inflated Amazon prices as "a price floor

86
00:04:42,934 --> 00:04:44,172
everywhere else"...

87
00:04:44,212 --> 00:04:46,863
which means, if the government is right,

88
00:04:46,903 --> 00:04:49,823
you pay the Amazon tax even at other
stores.

89
00:04:50,213 --> 00:04:55,585
Then there's Project Nessie — an algorithm
the FTC says predicted when competitors

90
00:04:55,625 --> 00:04:58,159
would match Amazon's price increases...

91
00:04:58,199 --> 00:05:01,657
so Amazon could raise prices and watch
rivals follow.

92
00:05:01,697 --> 00:05:06,805
The agency alleges it generated more than
one billion dollars in extra profit.

93
00:05:06,845 --> 00:05:10,434
Amazon says the FTC "grossly
mischaracterizes" the tool

94
00:05:10,474 --> 00:05:14,526
— and that it was scrapped because it
didn't work as intended.

95
00:05:14,566 --> 00:05:19,278
More broadly, Amazon says the lawsuit is
wrong on the facts and the law.

96
00:05:19,318 --> 00:05:22,314
But there's one cost we haven't counted
yet...

97
00:05:22,354 --> 00:05:25,218
the one that arrives after the sale is
over.

98
00:05:27,208 --> 00:05:29,984
Now for the cost that comes back in a box.

99
00:05:30,024 --> 00:05:32,935
According to the National Retail
Federation,

100
00:05:32,975 --> 00:05:36,421
roughly one in five online purchases gets
sent back.

101
00:05:36,461 --> 00:05:39,908
And in fashion, one kind of return has its
own name.

102
00:05:39,948 --> 00:05:40,646
Wardrobing.

103
00:05:40,686 --> 00:05:42,724
Buy an evening dress on Friday.

104
00:05:42,764 --> 00:05:44,937
Wear it to a wedding on Saturday.

105
00:05:44,977 --> 00:05:48,893
Return it on Monday — "didn't fit." In one
industry survey,

106
00:05:48,933 --> 00:05:52,179
six in ten retail executives said they'd
seen it.

107
00:05:52,219 --> 00:05:54,593
For the shopper, the return is free.

108
00:05:54,633 --> 00:05:59,823
For the seller, here's what one worn, one-
hundred-dollar dress actually costs.

109
00:06:00,213 --> 00:06:03,226
The customer gets their hundred dollars
back.

110
00:06:03,266 --> 00:06:06,076
Amazon refunds most of its referral fee...

111
00:06:06,116 --> 00:06:08,654
but keeps a refund administration fee.

112
00:06:08,694 --> 00:06:10,690
Three dollars and forty cents.

113
00:06:11,081 --> 00:06:14,843
The fulfillment fee Amazon charged to ship
the dress?

114
00:06:14,883 --> 00:06:15,776
Not refunded.

115
00:06:15,816 --> 00:06:17,210
About six dollars...

116
00:06:17,250 --> 00:06:17,569
gone.

117
00:06:17,959 --> 00:06:19,833
Then, a returns processing fee.

118
00:06:19,873 --> 00:06:21,439
Around three dollars more.

119
00:06:21,829 --> 00:06:26,947
And the dress itself comes back with makeup on
the collar and perfume in the fabric.

120
00:06:26,987 --> 00:06:28,421
It can't be sold as new.

121
00:06:28,461 --> 00:06:30,631
Twenty-eight dollars of inventory...

122
00:06:30,671 --> 00:06:31,368
written off.

123
00:06:31,757 --> 00:06:35,271
Total: a loss of about forty dollars and
forty cents

124
00:06:35,311 --> 00:06:37,595
— on a sale that no longer exists.

125
00:06:37,635 --> 00:06:41,423
At a ten percent margin, that's four full-
price sales...

126
00:06:41,463 --> 00:06:43,884
just to pay for one wardrobed dress.

127
00:06:43,924 --> 00:06:48,463
And even the sellers who survive returns
run into one more problem.

128
00:06:48,503 --> 00:06:48,805
Time.

129
00:06:50,795 --> 00:06:54,131
So far, that's fifty-two dollars in fees
and ads...

130
00:06:54,171 --> 00:06:57,109
and forty more every time a dress comes
back.

131
00:06:57,149 --> 00:06:59,558
But the last trap isn't a fee at all.

132
00:06:59,598 --> 00:07:01,941
Selling on Amazon starts with a bet.

133
00:07:01,981 --> 00:07:04,323
Sellers pay their factories up front

134
00:07:04,363 --> 00:07:08,824
— often two or three months before a
single unit reaches a customer.

135
00:07:09,215 --> 00:07:13,512
Then, when the sale finally happens, the
money still doesn't arrive.

136
00:07:13,552 --> 00:07:18,614
Under Amazon's current rules, seller funds
generally become available seven days

137
00:07:18,654 --> 00:07:21,165
after the customer's confirmed delivery.

138
00:07:21,205 --> 00:07:26,139
Add settlement cycles on top, and many
sellers wait two to four weeks to touch

139
00:07:26,179 --> 00:07:27,287
their own revenue.

140
00:07:27,677 --> 00:07:30,514
Meanwhile, the fees keep multiplying.

141
00:07:30,554 --> 00:07:36,966
Since 2024, there's a fee for how your boxes
get spread across Amazon's warehouses.

142
00:07:37,006 --> 00:07:42,564
And in 2026, a three-and-a-half percent
surcharge landed on top of every

143
00:07:42,604 --> 00:07:43,808
fulfillment fee.

144
00:07:44,197 --> 00:07:46,063
And then there's the corridor.

145
00:07:46,103 --> 00:07:50,639
Keep too much stock for too long, and you
pay aged inventory surcharges.

146
00:07:50,679 --> 00:07:54,960
But let your stock fall below roughly
twenty-eight days of supply...

147
00:07:55,000 --> 00:07:58,773
and you pay a low-inventory-level fee on
every unit shipped.

148
00:07:58,813 --> 00:08:00,108
Too much costs money.

149
00:08:00,148 --> 00:08:01,569
Too little costs money.

150
00:08:01,609 --> 00:08:03,476
Amazon collects on both sides.

151
00:08:03,516 --> 00:08:03,793
So...

152
00:08:03,833 --> 00:08:05,128
why does anyone stay?

153
00:08:07,118 --> 00:08:11,219
On your own website, the payment processor
takes about three dollars on a

154
00:08:11,259 --> 00:08:12,353
hundred-dollar sale.

155
00:08:12,393 --> 00:08:13,715
Sounds like a bargain...

156
00:08:13,755 --> 00:08:16,607
until you realize the customers don't come
with it.

157
00:08:16,647 --> 00:08:20,691
You have to buy them — one click at a time
— on Google, Meta, or TikTok.

158
00:08:20,731 --> 00:08:21,939
A different auction...

159
00:08:21,979 --> 00:08:23,641
with the same tollbooth logic.

160
00:08:23,681 --> 00:08:25,853
And Amazon does deliver something real.

161
00:08:25,893 --> 00:08:28,916
Over two hundred million Prime members,
fast delivery,

162
00:08:28,956 --> 00:08:31,866
and a checkout button people trust without
thinking.

163
00:08:31,906 --> 00:08:33,851
That's what sellers are paying for.

164
00:08:34,241 --> 00:08:36,229
What's changed is the price.

165
00:08:36,269 --> 00:08:40,720
In 2016, Amazon's cut was about a third of
a seller's revenue.

166
00:08:40,760 --> 00:08:42,966
By 2022, it was more than half.

167
00:08:43,006 --> 00:08:48,761
And in 2027, a federal judge in Seattle
will start deciding whether parts of how

168
00:08:48,801 --> 00:08:50,934
Amazon got here broke the law.

169
00:08:50,974 --> 00:08:54,556
If the FTC wins, the Buy Box rules could
change...

170
00:08:54,596 --> 00:08:57,598
and with them, prices across the internet.

171
00:08:57,638 --> 00:09:00,206
If Amazon wins, the tollbooth stays.

172
00:09:00,597 --> 00:09:00,858
So...

173
00:09:00,898 --> 00:09:02,361
back to where we started.

174
00:09:02,401 --> 00:09:05,850
When you spend one hundred dollars with a
seller on Amazon

175
00:09:05,890 --> 00:09:07,714
— who actually gets your money?

176
00:09:07,754 --> 00:09:11,383
About ten dollars goes to the business
that made the product.

177
00:09:11,423 --> 00:09:14,751
About fifty-two goes to the company that
owns the shelf.

178
00:09:14,791 --> 00:09:19,081
In modern commerce, the company that makes
the product rarely gets rich.

179
00:09:19,121 --> 00:09:23,832
The fortune belongs to whoever controls
the last three seconds before checkout.
